      "card_ids": ["C11"],
      "claim": "Cyproconazole, another triazole antifungal, was detected in tadpole brain (3% distribution), providing class-level evidence that triazole antifungal agents can cross the blood-brain barrier.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "Pass. The closest analogue SCH39304 achieved 57% CSF/serum penetration in animals, including during meningeal inflammation, and the query shares the key triazole-difluorophenyl carbinol pharmacophore; additional azole-class brain detection supports meaningful CNS access.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct human or query-specific CSF/brain PK data; extrapolation relies on a close analogue and class evidence.",
    "Efflux transporter profile of the query is not explicitly reported, though the high CSF penetration of the close analogue suggests net CNS access is not precluded.",
    "Meningitis may increase BBB permeability; the analogue data include inflamed meninges, but exact query exposure under the specified condition is not directly measured."
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [],
